The '''Constitution of Sinchew''' is the fundamental law of the state established at the end of the [[Sinchewese Revolutionary War]] in 1733. It constituted the '''Union of Sinchew, Hahkinawa and Mahik peoples''', whose short form is '''Sinchew''', and it replaced the colonial administration of the [[Sinchew Colony]].

It was drafted over eight months by the [[Sinchew Liberal Assembly|Liberal Assembly]] and the [[Union of Free Peoples]], the two bodies that had made the revolution, and it was written to be amended.

The document has been described since as the settlement of a revolution that the smallest class won. The Tianist population was about six per cent of the colony in 1725 and held most of its capital, and the constitution's institutions — the boundaries of the sixteen provinces, the equal representation of provinces in the Senate, and the election of senators by provincial assemblies — gave it a permanent majority in one chamber of a state in which it was a small minority.

== Name ==

The state was named the Union of Sinchew, Hahkinawa and Mahik peoples. The three names are those of the peoples the Union was understood to comprise: Sinchew, the population of the former colony; Hahkinawa, the Hahkina of the annexed province; and Mahik, the indigenous peoples of the region.

The Sitonglan, the emancipated population of northern Fosian origin, were not named. They were granted citizenship under the provisions of the constitution and were not constituted as one of the Union's peoples. Their number in 1725 was 115,318, and they were the largest population in the country not named in its title.

== Citizenship ==

Citizenship was extended to any person born in Sinchew and to any person who had lived in Sinchew since the revolution. The second provision admitted the adult population of the colony at the time the constitution took effect, including the populations the colonial returns had classified as Hahkina, Mahik, Sitonglan and enslaved.

The constitution made no distinction between the peoples named in its title and those it did not name. The Sitonglan were citizens under the same clause as everyone else born in the country, and the omission of their name from the title was an omission and not a restriction of status.

== Provinces ==

The constitution divided the country into sixteen provinces. Each province has an assembly of its own, and each elects two members of the Senate.

The boundaries were drawn by the Tianist minority in the assembly, which held most of the colony's capital. The effect is a permanent disproportion between the two halves of the country: the eleven provinces of the west and centre hold about 31 per cent of the national territory and elect twenty-two of the thirty-two senators, and the five provinces of the former Hachinawa hold about 69 per cent of the territory and elect ten.

Each province bears a name in both of the Union's written languages. Names are rendered phonetically in the script that receives them, and ordinary words semantically, so the two columns are not translations of one another.

