The provinces of the former Hachinawa are large because much of their territory was not under the republic's administration when the boundaries were drawn. The largest, Hashawa, contains chiefdoms that had never been subject to the colony, and it was constituted as a single province rather than divided because its settled districts were few.

The provinces of the west and centre are small for the opposite reason. Their boundaries were drawn where the administration's authority was complete and where the Tianist population was concentrated, and the equal representation of provinces in the Senate converts that concentration into a majority.

== General Assembly ==

{{Main|General Assembly of Sinchew}}

The lower house was the General Assembly, elected by universal male suffrage with members holding two-year terms and no property qualification for the vote. Seats were allocated at large from the provinces.

The two-year term made the Assembly the most frequently renewed body in the government. The constitution assigned it legislation, taxation, the appointment of the president and the appointment of the judges of the High Court.

The franchise was male. Women were excluded, and the exclusion was maintained for longer than any other provision was left unamended.

== Senate ==

The upper house was the Senate, composed of two members from each province, elected by the provincial assemblies.

The Senate was therefore elected at one remove from the population, and the provincial assemblies that chose its members were themselves elected within boundaries drawn by the Tianist minority. The arrangement gave that minority control of the Senate through two mechanisms rather than one: the equal representation of provinces regardless of size, and the smaller electorates of the small provinces, whose assemblies are easier for a concentrated class to carry.

== President ==

The executive was a President elected by the General Assembly. The president was not elected by the population and held no mandate independent of the Assembly that appointed him.

== High Court ==

The constitution established a High Court with inferior courts beneath it. The judges of the High Court were appointed by the General Assembly and held lifetime positions.

The lifetime tenure made the judiciary the only branch not subject to the Assembly's renewal, and it placed the court outside the two-year cycle at the same time as it placed its members there by election of the Assembly.

== Religion ==

The constitution provided for religious freedom and established no state religion.

The provision ended the position the [[Baiduism|Baiduist]] population had held under the colonial administration, which had been Tianist and had expelled the Baiduists of mainland Zong on religious grounds in 1606. It also ended the establishment of Tianism, which had been the religion of the colony's administration and of the Zong crown.

The provision was the point on which the two bodies that wrote the constitution agreed most closely. The [[Union of Free Peoples]] was Baiduist and Hahkina and had been subject to a religious establishment; the [[Sinchew Liberal Assembly|Liberal Assembly]] was Tianist and had lost the establishment that had favoured it. Neither had reason to maintain one.

== Language ==

The constitution named no official language. It recognised a set of languages for common use and provided that proceedings at the national level would be conducted in Zong, and that each province may designate its own secondary languages for provincial government.

The provision left the Union's three constituent peoples governed in the language of one of them at the national level and permitted the others to govern themselves in their own. Zong is the language of the former colony and of the Baiduists and Sitonglan; Hachina is the language of the former Hachinawa; and the Mahik and Apyãwa peoples retain their own.

== Rights ==

The constitution enumerated protections against the government: freedom of the press, freedom of assembly, {{w|habeas corpus}}, and freedom of religion.

The provisions were sought by both founding constituencies. The [[Sinchew Liberal Assembly|Liberal Assembly]] was a body of landowners who had been suppressed after the rebellion of 1717, and the Baiduist population had been expelled from Zong on religious grounds in 1606 and governed by an administration of another faith for a century.

== Land ==

The constitution did not redistribute land. It guaranteed the protection of land held by its owners against interference by the government, and provided that the government may purchase land at fair rates.

The provision settled the question left open by the revolution. The land settlement of the former Hachinawa had transferred the province's cultivable ground to Zong settlers through legal adjudication between 1662 and 1680, and the constitution confirmed the titles that transfer had created. The Hahkina and Baiduist constituencies of the [[Union of Free Peoples]], which had lost that ground, obtained the guarantee against further dispossession rather than the return of what had been taken.

== Debt ==

The constitution repudiated the colonial debts. The slave-backed loans issued by the colonial government in 1711 were voided, and the state assumed no obligation to compensate the landowners who had contributed enslaved labour to the war.

The landowning class accepted the repudiation as part of the settlement. It had been the immediate cause of the revolution: the Zong court had refused to honour the loans in 1717 and had required the colony to emancipate the remainder at its own cost, and the colony's leading class had joined the revolution rather than the crown.

== National and provincial powers ==

The constitution separated the powers of the national and provincial governments. The national government is bound by the constitution and holds the powers the document enumerates. The provinces hold the powers the constitution does not mention, and each has an assembly of its own to exercise them.

The arrangement is a residual-powers settlement: the national government is a government of enumerated authority and the provinces are governments of general authority. It was the provision the former imperial province sought, and it is the reason the abolition of the province's autonomy was accepted — the assembly of the former Hachinawa was dissolved into the General Assembly, and every province received an assembly of the same kind.

== Royal house ==

The royal house of the former Hachinawa was abolished. The dynasty that had reigned since before the first Zong sighting of the coast in 1458, and which had administered the annexed province until the death of [[Kayatsi]] in 1678, ceased to be a house of any constitutional status.

The state is named for the Hahkina among its constituent peoples, and it contains no office for the house that had ruled them.

== Amendment ==

The constitution was drafted over eight months and written to be amended, and the procedure has been used throughout the state's history.

